  three are you gonna be stuffing  hamburgers in your face at 259. like  it's not totally sustainable right um  but people actually did see an  improvement in mood which was  interesting in the 14 weeks and look  you're losing weight you're feeling good  but when it comes down to maintaining it  most of these people said it wouldn't  really be something that would be  effective in the long term and only  eight people said they could stick to  the 7 a.m to 3 p.m window which is  really interesting right because their  parents eating with their kids there are  shift workers who work overnight i  thought it was so interesting you  pointed out the increased mood  effects and also a reduction in blood  pressure why would that be yeah so with  blood pressure we know as your weight  increases your blood pressure increases  as well and even losing as much as 10  pounds can really make considerable  differences in your blood pressure um so  there definitely is incentive with that  but weight is not the ultimate  determinant of health and i really want  to stress that right there are so many  other things like your cholesterol your  blood sugar levels that really impact  our health in the long term and think  about 7 a.m to 3 p.m are you eating junk  food the whole time or are you eating  nutritious wholesome foods right that's  really what it boils down to in terms of  longevity how much of this is just about  following a system any system over a  prolonged period of time i think  intermittent fasting is so um  attractive to so many people because  it's just start eating at this time and  stop eating at this time you're not  tracking calories you're not counting  macros right but maybe you can take the  research and the findings from the study  and implement certain things into your  own life right so maybe you say okay at  8 p.m i'm going to stop eating for the  rest of the night and cut out that late  night snacking right or i'm not going to  eat overnight that's a big thing back  when i was in private practice a lot of  people eat in the middle of the night  it's a huge issue  don't people sleep in the middle  they go to bed and they wake up and wake  up for a snack and then they go back  like you've never i sleep right through  okay all right i think you know look i i  have a young baby so i'm up in the  middle of the night right and so  scenario you're tempted to maybe grab  something from the fridge  it's very very common but it really is  important to try and keep that overnight  fast because that's going to help you  sleep better improve your mood your  weight all the above okay so when it  comes to these time restricting diets or  eating schedules um how do you do it in  a healthy way yeah so i think if you  look at your particular lifestyle your  job for example right you guys are  probably waking up super early so maybe  this winter could work for you but  someone who's at a normal nine to five  maybe you put something like 12 to 8 or  10 to 6 maybe a better window um and  ultimately like i said it's about the  quality of the foods you're eating i  can't stress that enough right  making sure that you're filling that  window with nutritious wholesome foods  to help you prevent chronic disease and  really calories are not just a calorie  the quality of those calories matters  and you've got to think about what it's  doing to the earth right nate that's  true you heard my top of the table all  right stephanie deputy uh director of  nutrition at good housekeeping thank you  very much thank you guys
 

